// CurveFit — log-log least squares. Six points on a doubling ladder in, one growth exponent out.
//
// The question the whole scale suite asks is "how does this phase's cost grow with program size?",
// and the honest way to answer it is to assume the cost obeys a power law
//
//     cost = C * size^k
//
// and recover `k`. Take logs and it is a straight line — `log cost = log C + k * log size` — so `k`
// is the SLOPE of an ordinary least-squares fit through the log-log points. k ~ 1.0 is linear and
// k ~ 2.0 is quadratic.
//
// `k` IS THE NUMBER THE INSTRUMENT EXISTS TO PRODUCE, and it is worth being precise about why it is
// worth recording rather than checking. The ALLOCATION exponent is exact: allocation counts are
// bit-for-bit reproducible, so the same compiler on the same corpus fits the same slope to the last
// digit, on an idle machine or a loaded one. The TIME exponent reproduces to about 1%. Neither is a
// good boolean — no threshold separates "fine" from "not fine" — and both are excellent TRACKED
// numbers: a phase going superlinear shows up as its exponent climbing down the pages of
// `docs/optimization-log.md`, with no threshold needed at all.
//
// WHY A FIT AND NOT A RATIO. The tempting cheap answer is to divide the top rung by the bottom and
// take a log: `k = log2(cost5 / cost0) / 5`. That uses two of six measurements and throws away the
// four in between — so a phase that is linear at every rung except one, where it hitches, reports
// as perfectly linear, and a single jumpy endpoint moves the answer on its own. The fit uses every
// point, and it also produces a RESIDUAL.
//
// WHAT THE RESIDUAL ACTUALLY MEANS — and what it does NOT. It is the RMS distance of the points
// from the fitted line in log-space: how well the cost obeys a SINGLE power law. It was once read as
// a noise detector ("scattered points mean a busy machine") and gated on. That inference is
// unsound, and the proof is in the numbers this file produces: the ALLOCATION curves cannot move —
// their points are bit-for-bit reproducible — and the whole-compile one still carries a residual of
// ~0.090. A nonzero residual therefore does not imply a loaded machine. What it usually implies is
// that the curve is a SUM of power laws with different exponents, which is not a power law at all:
// it bends, from the shallower exponent at small sizes toward the steeper one at large ones, on a
// perfectly idle machine. `regalloc:liveness` is exactly that (`timedLiveness` bills two call sites
// into one bucket — one liveness per function, and one after every split), and so is any parent
// curve over its children.
//
// So the residual is REPORTED, as what it is: a measure of how well one exponent describes this
// curve. It renders no verdict, and nothing in this suite branches on it.

// A growth exponent, and a fit's residual, in fixed-point THOUSANDTHS. There is no float formatting
// on this path and no reason to pull one in: 1.037 is `1037`. The arithmetic is done in floats
// (Math.log needs them) and only the RESULT is quantized, so the quantization is a display and
// comparison convenience, never an accumulated error.
export typealias Milli = int(i64.min to i64.max)

let MilliScale = 1000.0
let MilliScaleInt = 1000

// How an absent number renders, and the only spelling of it. It appears wherever a curve could not
// be fitted — in the report, and in the trend log, out of which it is read back again.
//
// It SAYS "not available" rather than looking like a value. A bare `-` was the obvious alternative
// and it is a trap: a phase whose cost SHRINKS as the program grows has a negative exponent and
// renders as `-1.037`, so the eye — and any future parser less careful than this one — has to know
// that one dash means "no data" and the same dash followed by digits means the opposite.
//
// ⚠ It contains a SLASH, and the trend log's exponent cell is `<time> / <allocations>`. That cell is
// therefore split on `" / "` INCLUDING THE SPACES (ScaleHistory.ExponentCellSeparator), because
// splitting `n/a / n/a` on a bare `/` yields four fields and turns the log into a document this
// instrument can write and cannot read.
export let UnfittableText = "n/a"

// The outcome of fitting one curve. A union, not a struct with an `ok` flag, so an unfittable curve
// cannot be read as an exponent of zero — which is what a struct would have handed back, and which
// would have read as "perfectly flat".
export union FitResult
	// `exponent` is the slope, `residual` the RMS error of the fit in log-space.
	fitted(exponent Milli, residual Milli)
	// Fewer than two usable points, or a curve that is entirely zeros. NOT an exponent of 0:
	// there is nothing here to fit, and saying so is different from saying it is flat.
	unfittable(reason String)

	// The exponent as the report and the trend log print it — `UnfittableText` when there is none.
	export function exponentText() returns String
		return match self 'f'
			unfittable gives UnfittableText
			fitted(exponent, _) gives milliText(exponent)
		end 'f'
	end 'exponentText'

	export function residualText() returns String
		return match self 'f'
			unfittable gives UnfittableText
			fitted(_, residual) gives milliText(residual)
		end 'f'
	end 'residualText'
end 'FitResult'

// `Real` is stdlib Math's own float alias (`float(f64.min to f64.max)`) — the type `Math.log` and
// `Math.pow` take. Using it rather than declaring a private twin keeps the fit's numbers assignable
// to the functions that consume them without a cast at every call.
export typealias FitPointArray = Array with Real

// Fit `cost = C * size^k` and return `k` with the fit's residual.
//
// `sizes` and `costs` are parallel; both must be positive to have a logarithm. A point whose cost
// is ZERO is DROPPED, not clamped to one: a phase that did no measurable work at a rung has no
// information to contribute about its growth, and clamping it to 1 ns would fabricate a data point
// and bend the curve toward whatever the clamp implied.
export function fitPowerLaw(sizes FitPointArray, costs FitPointArray) returns FitResult
	if sizes.count() != costs.count() 'mismatched'
		panic("fitPowerLaw: {sizes.count()} sizes but {costs.count()} costs — the arrays must be parallel")
	end 'mismatched'

	var logSizes = FitPointArray.create()
	var logCosts = FitPointArray.create()

	for i in 0 upto sizes.count() 'eachPoint'
		let size = try sizes.get(i) otherwise panic("fitPowerLaw: sizes.get OOB at {i}")
		let cost = try costs.get(i) otherwise panic("fitPowerLaw: costs.get OOB at {i}")
		if size <= 0.0 or cost <= 0.0 'noLogarithm'
			continue
		end 'noLogarithm'
		logSizes.push(Math.log(size))
		logCosts.push(Math.log(cost))
	end 'eachPoint'

	let n = logSizes.count()
	if n < 2 'tooFewPoints'
		return FitResult.unfittable("only {n} rung(s) did measurable work — a growth exponent needs at least two")
	end 'tooFewPoints'

	return leastSquaresSlope(logSizes, logCosts: logCosts)
end 'fitPowerLaw'

// Ordinary least squares through the log-log points: slope = Sxy / Sxx, intercept = ybar - slope*xbar.
function leastSquaresSlope(logSizes FitPointArray, logCosts FitPointArray) returns FitResult
	let n = logSizes.count()

	var sumX = 0.0
	var sumY = 0.0
	for i in 0 upto n 'sums'
		sumX = sumX + (try logSizes.get(i) otherwise panic("leastSquaresSlope: logSizes.get OOB at {i}"))
		sumY = sumY + (try logCosts.get(i) otherwise panic("leastSquaresSlope: logCosts.get OOB at {i}"))
	end 'sums'
	let meanX = sumX / n
	let meanY = sumY / n

	var sxy = 0.0
	var sxx = 0.0
	for i in 0 upto n 'covariance'
		let dx = (try logSizes.get(i) otherwise panic("leastSquaresSlope: logSizes.get OOB at {i}")) - meanX
		let dy = (try logCosts.get(i) otherwise panic("leastSquaresSlope: logCosts.get OOB at {i}")) - meanY
		sxy = sxy + dx * dy
		sxx = sxx + dx * dx
	end 'covariance'

	// Every rung is a different size, so the log-sizes are distinct and Sxx cannot be zero — unless
	// every surviving point came from ONE rung, which `fitPowerLaw`'s n>=2 check cannot rule out
	// (two points from the same rung is impossible here, but the guard costs nothing and a division
	// by zero would produce a silent infinity, which would be reported as an exponent).
	if sxx == 0.0 'noSpread'
		return FitResult.unfittable("every usable rung was the same size — nothing to fit a growth curve against")
	end 'noSpread'

	let slope = sxy / sxx
	let intercept = meanY - slope * meanX

	return FitResult.fitted(toMilli(slope), residual: toMilli(rmsResidual(logSizes, logCosts: logCosts, slope: slope, intercept: intercept)))
end 'leastSquaresSlope'

// Root-mean-square distance of the points from the fitted line, in log-space.
//
// This is the number that separates "the compiler is superlinear" from "the machine was busy". A
// genuine power law — even a bad one — lies ON a log-log line, so its residual is small no matter
// how steep it is. Scattered points do not, however plausible the slope through them looks.
function rmsResidual(logSizes FitPointArray, logCosts FitPointArray, slope Real, intercept Real) returns Real
	let n = logSizes.count()
	var sumSquares = 0.0
	for i in 0 upto n 'eachPoint'
		let x = try logSizes.get(i) otherwise panic("rmsResidual: logSizes.get OOB at {i}")
		let y = try logCosts.get(i) otherwise panic("rmsResidual: logCosts.get OOB at {i}")
		let error = y - (intercept + slope * x)
		sumSquares = sumSquares + error * error
	end 'eachPoint'
	return sqrt(sumSquares / n)
end 'rmsResidual'

// A float to fixed-point thousandths, rounded to NEAREST — so 1.0299 reads as 1.030, not 1.029.
//
// `trunc` is the only float->int route the language has (`as` widens int->float and never narrows),
// and it truncates TOWARD ZERO. Adding half a unit before truncating is what turns that into
// round-to-nearest, and the half has to carry the value's sign or a negative exponent would round
// the wrong way: trunc(-1.037 * 1000 - 0.5) is -1037, while trunc(-1037.4) alone would be -1037 only
// by luck and -1036 for -1036.6.
function toMilli(value Real) returns Milli
	let scaled = value * MilliScale
	if scaled < 0.0 'negative'
		return trunc(scaled - 0.5)
	end 'negative'
	return trunc(scaled + 0.5)
end 'toMilli'

// Fixed-point thousandths rendered for a human: `1037` -> `1.037`.
export function milliText(value Milli) returns String
	if value < 0 'negative'
		return "-{milliText(0 - value)}"
	end 'negative'

	let whole = value / MilliScaleInt
	let frac = value mod MilliScaleInt
	return "{whole}.{zeroPad3(frac)}"
end 'milliText'

// The fractional part of a milli value, always three digits — `7` is `007`, not `7`, or `1.007`
// would print as `1.7` and read as ten times its actual value.
function zeroPad3(frac Milli) returns String
	if frac < 10 'oneDigit'
		return "00{frac}"
	end 'oneDigit'
	if frac < 100 'twoDigits'
		return "0{frac}"
	end 'twoDigits'
	return "{frac}"
end 'zeroPad3'

// `milliText`'s inverse: `1.037` -> `1037`. The exponents this suite records go into
// `docs/optimization-log.md` as text and come back out of it on the next run, to be differenced
// against — so the round trip has to exist, and it has to live beside the renderer it must agree
// with rather than in whichever file happened to need it first.
//
// Exactly three fractional digits are demanded, because that is what `milliText` writes. Accepting
// `1.03` would have to decide whether it meant 1.030 or 1.003, and a trend log is the last place to
// guess.
export function parseMilliText(raw String) returns Milli throws ScaleError
	if raw.startsWith("-") 'negative'
		return 0 - (try parseMilliText(raw.replace("-", with: "")))
	end 'negative'

	let parts = raw.split(".")
	if parts.count() != 2 'notFixedPoint'
		throw ScaleError.reason("'{raw}' is not a fixed-point thousandths value (expected <whole>.<3 digits>, as milliText writes)")
	end 'notFixedPoint'

	let whole = try parts.get(0) otherwise panic("parseMilliText: split gave 2 parts but get(0) failed")
	let frac = try parts.get(1) otherwise panic("parseMilliText: split gave 2 parts but get(1) failed")
	if frac.count() != MilliFractionDigits 'wrongPrecision'
		throw ScaleError.reason("'{raw}' has {frac.count()} fractional digit(s); milliText always writes {MilliFractionDigits}, and fewer would not say whether .03 meant .030 or .003")
	end 'wrongPrecision'

	// The whole part is REJECTED before it is scaled, not after. `parseCount` accepts anything up to
	// `u64.max`, and multiplying such a value by {MilliScaleInt} to reach thousandths overflows `Milli`
	// — which TRAPS on its range rather than throwing. The log is a document a human edits, so a
	// nonsense exponent in it is bad input and must come back as a ScaleError naming the file, never as
	// a panic in a fitting routine.
	if whole.count() > MilliWholeMaxDigits 'wouldOverflow'
		throw ScaleError.reason("'{raw}' has a {whole.count()}-digit whole part; no growth exponent is that number, and scaling it to thousandths would overflow rather than be rejected")
	end 'wouldOverflow'

	return (try parseCount(whole)) * MilliScaleInt + (try parseCount(frac))
end 'parseMilliText'

let MilliFractionDigits = 3

// Fifteen digits, times a thousand, is 10^18 — inside i64, with room to spare for the fraction added
// to it. It is a bound on the ARITHMETIC, deliberately, and not a claim about how large an exponent
// this suite considers sane: a curve going quadratic is the thing being watched for, and an
// instrument that refused to read back a number because it disapproved of it would be the gauge
// deciding what the engine is allowed to have done.
let MilliWholeMaxDigits = 15
